<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day22" xml:id="day-22-lecture">
  <title>Day 22</title>

  <introduction>

    <p>
        8.1: Decision Trees
    </p>
  </introduction>

<objectives>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>Understand how a decision tree makes decisions.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Given a tree and a data point with multiple predictors <m>(x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_p)</m>, derive the predicted outcome <m>\hat{y}</m>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Do this for both regression and classification decision trees.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Identify the different parts of a decision tree and explain what they represent.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Identify leaves (terminal nodes), internal nodes, and branches (edges).</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Given a simple example (e.g., two predictors), identify which regions of the predictor space map to each leaf.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Understand how to fit a decision tree.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Identify the cost function for regression and classification trees.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Explain how recursive binary splitting works.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Explain when and how the splitting process ends.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Identify the meta-parameters of decision trees that determine their flexibility.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Explain why decision trees need to be pruned.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Compare the advantages and disadvantages of decision trees and linear regression.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Big idea</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Previously, we've always had some form of function for prediction: <md>f(X_1,\ldots,X_p)=\hat{Y}</md>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Now, we make a sequence of decisions to make a prediction, either for regression or classification.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Recall: regression means the outcome is quantitative, while classification means the outcome is qualitative.</p>
        </li>        
      </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/binarytree-faces-w800.png">
        <shortdescription>A binary decision tree using modal verbs Should, Could, Would, and Did leading to various emotional faces.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        Image: <url href="https://marekbennett.com/2014/02/14/decision-tree/">Decision tree illustration</url>.
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Subset of Hitters data</title>
  <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
    <image source="figures/Hitters-TreeSlides.png">
      <shortdescription>Table displaying baseball hitter data with columns for Hits, Years, Salary, and LogSalary.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Remove observations with missing salary values.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Log-transform salary to obtain a distribution that is closer to a bell shape.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Goal: predict log salary. We can reverse the transformation by returning <m>\exp(x)</m> if the model returns <m>x</m>.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>First decision tree example</title>
  <sidebyside widths="25% 45%">
    <image source="figures/Hitters-TreeSlides-OnlyLog.png">
      <shortdescription>Table displaying baseball hitter data with columns for Hits, Years, and LogSalary.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    
    <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
      <shortdescription>Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and Hits to produce three leaf nodes.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
  
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Top split assigns observations with <m>Years &lt; 4.5</m> (in major leagues) to the left branch.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Return the mean response for players in that group.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Predictions:</p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>Mean log salary is 5.107, so the predicted salary is <m>\exp(5.107)=\$165,174</m> thousand dollars.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>5.999 \Rightarrow \$402,834</m>.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>6.740 \Rightarrow \$845,346</m>.</p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Interpretation of example</title>
  <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
      <shortdescription>Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and Hits to produce three leaf nodes.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Years is the most important factor for determining salary.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Players with less experience earn lower salaries than more experienced players.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For less experienced players, the number of hits plays little role in salary.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For more experienced players, the number of hits affects salary.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>This is likely an oversimplification of the real relationship, but it is easier to interpret and has a nice graphical representation.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Regions defined by the tree</title>
  <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
      <shortdescription>Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and Hits to produce three leaf nodes.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Note that the tree is is drawn <q>upside-down</q></p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Leaves (terminal nodes) of the tree:</p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p><m>R_1=\{X\mid Years&lt;4.5\}</m></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>R_2=\{X\mid Years\geq 4.5,\ Hits&lt;117.5\}</m></p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p><m>R_3=\{X\mid Years\geq 4.5,\ Hits\geq 117.5\}</m></p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Other splits are called internal nodes.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Segments that connect nodes are called branches or edges.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Viewing Regions Defined by Tree</title>
  <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
      <shortdescription>Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and Hits to produce three leaf nodes.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <image source="figures/Fig8-2.png">
      <shortdescription>Scatter plot of Hits versus Years partitioned into three regions R1, R2, and R3 by a regression tree.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>How do we actually get the tree? Two steps</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>We divide the predictor space -- that is, the set of possible values for <m>X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_p</m> -- into <m>J</m> distinct and non-overlapping regions, <m>R_1, R_2, \ldots, R_J</m>.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>For every observation that falls into the region <m>R_j</m>, we make the same prediction: the mean of the response values for the training observations in <m>R_j</m>.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

    </stack>
    <image source="figures/Fig8-3b.png">
      <shortdescription>A two-dimensional feature space partitioned into five rectangular regions R1 through R5 by vertical and horizontal splits.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Step 1: How do we decide on <m>R_j</m>s?</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack>
      <p><bold>Goal:</bold></p>
      <p>Find boxes <m>R_1,\ldots,R_J</m> that minimize</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \sum_{j=1}^J \sum_{i \in R_j} (y_i-\hat y_{R_j})^2
        </md>
      </p>
      <p><m>\hat y_{R_j}</m> = mean response for training observations in the <m>j</m>th box.</p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Cost is just RSS.</p></li>
          <li><p>Can't actually check every possible partition.</p></li>
          <li><p>Instead, use a top-down greedy approach called <m>\text{Recursive binary splitting}</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Begins at the top of the tree with all data points.</p></li>
          <li><p>Uses the best split at every step.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Recursive Binary Splitting</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack>
      <p>One split:</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Pick <m>X_j</m> and cutpoint <m>s</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Choose them so that splitting into <m>\{X \mid X_j &lt; s\}</m> and <m>\{X \mid X_j \geq s\}</m> results in the largest possible reduction in RSS.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Note here we look at all combinations of <m>X_j</m> and <m>s</m> for each split of the tree.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
          R_1(j,s) &amp;= \{X \mid X_j &lt; s\}\\
          R_2(j,s) &amp;= \{X \mid X_j \geq s\}
          \end{aligned}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
          &amp;\sum_{i \mid x_i \in R_1(j,s)}(y_i - \hat y_{R_1})^2\\
          &amp;+\sum_{i \mid x_i \in R_2(j,s)}(y_i - \hat y_{R_2})^2
          \end{aligned}
        </md>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Rinse and repeat</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">

    <image source="figures/Fig8-3c.png">
      <shortdescription>Decision tree diagram illustrating the sequence of binary splits on X1 and X2 resulting in regions R1 through R5.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <image source="figures/Fig8-3b.png">
      <shortdescription>A two-dimensional feature space partitioned into five rectangular regions R1 through R5 by vertical and horizontal splits.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
      
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Do this repeatedly.</p></li>
          <li><p>Each time, we can split one of the previously identified regions.</p></li>
          <li><p>Keep going until some stopping criterion is reached. For example, continue until each region has at most 5 observations.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Pruning</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-4.png">
      <shortdescription>Complex regression tree for baseball player salaries featuring multiple splits on Years, Hits, RBI, Walks, Runs, and Putouts.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Big trees leave you open to potential overfitting.</p></li>
          <li><p>Could just stop building earlier, but that's short-sighted.</p></li>
          <li><p>Instead, grow a big tree and prune it back.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find the subtree with the best test error rate.</p></li>
          <li><p>There are too many subtrees to test them all.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Result of pruning</title>

    <image source="figures/Fig8-5.png" width="75%">
      <shortdescription>Plot of Mean Squared Error versus tree size for Training, Cross-Validation, and Test sets, including error bars for each.</shortdescription>
    </image>

      <sidebyside widths="49% 49%">
        <image source="figures/Fig8-4.png">
          <shortdescription>Complex regression tree for baseball player salaries featuring multiple splits on Years, Hits, RBI, Walks, Runs, and Putouts.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
          <shortdescription>Pruned regression tree with three terminal nodes.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Result of pruning is the three-leaf tree on the right. This example technically uses a more complex pruning method than the one described.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Decisions trees for classification: Basic idea</title>
  <sidebyside widths="35% 45%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-6c.png">
      <shortdescription>Classification tree for heart disease predicting Yes or No based on splits of Thal, Ca, MaxHR, and ChestPain.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>\hat p_{mk}</m> = proportion of training observations in <m>R_m</m> from the <m>k</m>th class.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p><m>E = 1-\max_k(\hat p_{mk})</m></p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li><p>Fraction of training observations not in the most common class.</p></li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Example from heart data.</p></li>
          <li><p>Can't use RSS; need an error rate.</p></li>
          <li><p>Could decide splits by classification error rate.</p></li>
          <li><p>Not necessarily the best approach. There are other options, but we will not discuss those in our class.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Example</title>
  <image source="figures/Fig8-6a.png">
    <shortdescription>Large, complex classification tree for heart disease predicting Yes or No based on numerous clinical features and splits.</shortdescription>
  </image>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>The terminal nodes here: Yes = person has heart disease; No = no heart disease.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Pruning the example</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 35%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-6b.png">
      <shortdescription>Plot of classification error versus tree size for Training, Cross-Validation, and Test sets, with error bars for each.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <image source="figures/Fig8-6c.png">
      <shortdescription>Classification tree for heart disease predicting Yes or No based on splits of Thal, Ca, MaxHR, and ChestPain.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Linear models vs trees</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/Fig8-7-top.png">
        <shortdescription>Two plots comparing a linear decision boundary with a decision tree approximation using axis-aligned rectangular splits in feature space.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Obviously, the linear model does better here.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/Fig8-7-bottom.png">
        <shortdescription>Two plots showing a decision tree perfectly capturing a rectangular boundary with axis-aligned splits, compared to a linear boundary.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Not going to beat this case, though.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Pros/Cons</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
    <stack>
      <p>Pros:</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Trees are very easy to explain to people. Often easier to explain than linear regression!</p></li>
          <li><p>Trees can be displayed graphically and are easily interpreted even by a non-expert, especially if they are small.</p></li>
          <li><p>Trees can easily handle qualitative predictors without the need to create dummy variables.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>Cons:</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Not as accurate as other methods of classification and regression.</p></li>
          <li><p>Not robust: a small change in the data can cause a large change in the estimated tree.</p></li>
          <li><p>Fix: aggregate many decision trees.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>TL;DR</title>
  <sidebyside widths="45% 40%">
    <image source="figures/Fig8-1.png">
      <shortdescription>Regression tree for baseball player salaries splitting on Years and Hits to produce three leaf nodes.</shortdescription>
    </image>
    <image source="figures/Fig8-2.png">
      <shortdescription>Scatter plot of Hits versus Years partitioned into three regions R1, R2, and R3 by a regression tree.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </sidebyside>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Split into regions by greedily decreasing RSS.</p></li>
          <li><p>Prune the tree using cost complexity.</p></li>
          <li><p>Not robust: next time, figure out how to aggregate trees.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
</paragraphs>

  </handout>